L000500	\section{Results}
L000501	
L000502	\subsection*{Capturing NLOS Signals with Consumer LiDAR}
L000503	
L000504	In our experiments, we use a portable smartphone LiDAR system with $\sim100$ pixels, each consisting of a co-located laser emitter and single-photon avalanche diode (SPAD) sensor. Each SPAD pixel shares an optical axis with exactly one laser spot. While the configuration is similar to the confocal capture setup used by O'Toole et al. \cite{o2018confocal}, our setup is integrated onto an existing smartphone-grade LiDAR. Because all pixels and laser spots are active simultaneously, we assume that the hidden object of interest has strong retroreflective properties in order to retain the image formation model of confocal scanning setups \cite{o2018confocal}. However, we empirically show that our model works on diffuse objects as well. We assume object motion is a rigid-body translation (no rotations). The camera is handheld and free to move in an unstructured manner, but must be aimed towards the relay surface (i.e. the virtual mirror) at $z=0$, as shown in \cref{fig:teaser}a. We refer to the imaged area on the wall as the virtual aperture. The camera captures signals from the hidden object by measuring light that travels from the laser $\rightarrow$ virtual aperture $\rightarrow$ hidden object $\rightarrow$ virtual aperture $\rightarrow$ SPAD pixel. Each pixel measures picosecond-scale light transport by recording the distribution of light intensity as a function of time. From these space-time measurements, we infer properties of the hidden object.
L000505	
L000506	
L000507	
L000508	\subsection*{Space-Time Impulse Response}
L000509	
L000510	The space-time impulse response (STIR) is a continuous-valued function $i(x, y, \tau)$ that fully describes the hidden scene. The STIR contains the temporal response $i(\tau)$ at every sampled $(x,y)$ location on the relay wall, assumed to be at $z=0$. Prior works have shown that the hidden scene can be estimated if the STIR is captured with sufficient SNR, spatio-temporal resolution, and virtual aperture size \cite{kirmani2009looking, gupta2012reconstruction}.
L000511	
L000512	Consumer LiDARs, however, capture a noisy, low-spatial-resolution, and cropped estimate of the STIR $\hat{i}(x, y, \tau)$. As a result, a single frame is not informative enough to reconstruct the hidden scene. However, if the hidden scene is static, we can leverage the portability of the LiDAR system by moving the camera and scanning the relay surface (as shown in \cref{fig:teaser}a). This camera motion results in increased synthetic aperture size and resolution due to additional spatial sampling of the STIR. Using multiple frames also improves SNR by exploiting redundancy in measurements. Once sufficiently sampled, the STIR can be used to reconstruct the hidden scene using techniques that don't rely on uniform spatial sampling \cite{velten2012recovering, liu2019non, liu2020phasor}. We show such a 3D reconstruction using filtered backprojection in \cref{fig:teaser}b.
L000513	
L000514	\begin{figure}
L000515	    \centering
L000516	
L000517	    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{final_figures/fig2.pdf}
L000518	    \caption{\textbf{Motion-induced aperture sampling model.} The space-time measurements can be expressed as the combined effect of object shape, object motion, and camera pose using the convolutional property of the light-cone transform (LCT). LCT aligns scene space and measurement space by transforming depth in scene space as $v_z=z^2$ and time in measurement space as $v_\tau=(c\tau/2)^2$.}
L000519	    \label{fig:methods}
L000520	\end{figure}
L000521	
L000522	
L000523	\begin{comment}
L000524	Non-line-of-sight imaging is the problem of recovering properties of a hidden scene by indirectly observing light (through reflections from a relay surface) that originated from the hidden scene. A pulsed laser illuminates a point on the relay surface. This illuminated point acts as a virtual light source which illuminates the hidden scene. Light interacts with the hidden object, returns to the relay surface, and is measured by a time-of-flight sensor (a LiDAR in our case).
L000525	
L000526	Prior works have shown that a hidden scene can be recovered if its space-time impulse response (STIR) \cite{kirmani2009looking, gupta2012reconstruction} is known. The STIR of a hidden scene is the temporal response $i(\tau)$ at every point $(x, y)$ on the relay surface. Although the STIR is a continuous function in space and time, in practice, the camera can only sample the STIR at a finite number of spatial locations (due to finite spatial resolution). Furthermore, these spots are often relatively close together (due to finite field of view), resulting in a limited synthetic aperture. These measurements will also be noisy due to practical constraints (i.e. low laser power, low exposure time).
L000527	
L000528	At a given frame index $t$, the camera captures a noisy, low-spatial-resolution, and spatially cropped measurement $\hat{i}_t(x, y, \tau)$ of the true STIR $i_t(x, y, \tau)$. Because the STIR is dependent on the position of the object, object motion results in a temporally varying STIR. The camera pose determines the spatial points that are observed on the relay surface at a given frame; therefore camera motion results in a temporally varying spatial sampling of the STIR. With these practical factors considered, the measurement at a given frame can be expressed as
L000529	
L000530	\begin{equation}
L000531	   \hat{i}_t(\mathbf{x}, \tau) = \sum_{\mathbf{x}_p \in \mathcal{P}} \delta(\mathbf{x}_p-\mathbf{K}\mathbf{P}_t\mathbf{x}) \cdot i_t(\mathbf{x},\tau),
L000532	    \label{eq:forward}
L000533	\end{equation}
L000534	
L000535	\noindent where $\mathcal{P}$ is the set containing all pixel coordinate locations $\mathbf{x}_p = \begin{bmatrix} x_p & y_p & 1 \end{bmatrix}^\top$ in the camera in homogeneous coordinates, $\mathbf{K}$ is the camera intrinsic matrix, $\mathbf{P}$ is the camera extrinsic matrix, and $\mathbf{x} = \begin{bmatrix} x & y & 0 & 1 \end{bmatrix}^\top$ represents a 2D point $(x, y)$ on the planar relay surface in homogeneous world coordinates (the plane is at $z=0$). \sid{Noise isn't exactly additive (Poisson noise but other noise (e.g. dark count) is)}. A point $\mathbf{x}$ on the relay surface is only measured if it maps to a pixel location on the camera. Prior works capture a sufficiently good approximation of $\tau(x, y, t)$ by obtaining sufficient spatial sampling (through judicious choice of the pixel locations $\mathcal{P}$) and sufficient signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) (through long exposures, static scenes and cameras, and high laser power). However, in our problem setting, $\mathcal{P}$ is fixed, and high laser powers, long exposure times, and static scenes are not practical, making it challenging to recover the per-frame STIR $i_t(\mathbf{x}, \tau)$.
L000536	
L000537	To address the challenges of cropping, sampling, and noise, we use information across multiple captured frames. Exploiting the motion of the object and camera allows us to sample different points on the canonical response over time, which allows us to synthetically obtain a larger aperture (e.g. SAR) and higher spatial sampling of the wall. By utilizing the redundancies across frames, we can also improve the overall SNR of the captured images,analagous to burst photography in low-light.
L000538	
L000539	\end{comment}
