L000843	\subsection*{Pre-Computing Canonical STIR and Rendering as an Indexing Operation}
L000844	
L000845	In \cref{eq:mas_model}, we showed that the object shape can be fully described by the continuous space-time impulse response (STIR) of the object. The relationship between the object shape and this canonical STIR is determined by the PSF kernel
L000846	
L000847	\begin{equation}
L000848	    h(x, y, v) = \delta(x^2 + y^2 - v),
L000849	\end{equation}
L000850	
L000851	\noindent which parametrizes a parabola in LCT-transformed space-time. As a result, the canonical STIR of the object can be approximate as a sum of parabolas
L000852	
L000853	\begin{equation}
L000854	    \mathcal{I}(x, y, v) \approx \sum_{p \in \mathcal{P}} \rho_p \cdot h(x-p_x, y-p_y,  v-p_v),
L000855	\end{equation}
L000856	
L000857	\noindent where $\mathcal{P}$ is the point cloud defining the object shape, $p=(p_x, p_y, p_z)$, $p_v=p_z^2$, and $\rho_p$ is the albedo at the $p$. We normalize the point cloud such that the centroid of the points is defined to be at the origin. We assume that the object has uniform albedo and set $\rho_p=1$.
L000858	
L000859	Under object translation, the object shape as viewed by the relay surface is time-invariant. As a result, we can pre-compute the canonical measurement $\mathcal{I}$ as a voxel cube $\hat{\mathcal{I}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_x \times N_y \times N_v}$, where $N_x$, $N_y$, and $N_v$ are the number of points sampled along the $x$, $y$, and $v$ direction of the canonical STIR. Once this STIR is pre-computed at pre-determined spatial-temporal points, any measurement can be efficiently rendered by simply indexing into the correct space-time points using \cref{eq:mas_model}.
L000860	
L000861	
L000862	
L000863	\subsection*{Details on Particle Filtering}
L000864	
L000865	We use $1000$ particles to parametrize the posterior distribution. The three main steps of particle filtering are particle propagation, evaluation, and resampling. Particle propagation requires specification of a motion model or prior, which we define to be
L000866	
L000867	\begin{equation}
L000868	    \mathbf{\Delta}_{t} \sim \mathcal{N}(\mathbf{\Delta}_{t-1}, r\mathbf{I}),
L000869	\end{equation}
L000870	
L000871	\noindent where $\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix, $\mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma)$ is a normal distribution with mean $\mathbf{\mu}$ and covariance $\mathbf{\Sigma}$, and $r$ is a measure of the maximum expected radius that the object is expected to travel between consecutive frames. The motion prior can also be based on second-order information (e.g. velocity, acceleration) but we find that a proximity prior with $r=5$ cm already works well empirically at $30$ Hz. The particle evaluation step requires comparison of the rendered measurement $\hat{i}_{t, k} \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x \times n_y \times n_v}$ of the $k$th particle with the actual measurement $i_t \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x \times n_y \times n_t}$. We use a normalized dot product score function
L000872	
L000873	\begin{equation}
L000874	    \text{score}_k =  \left[ \frac{i_t \odot \hat{i}_{t, k}}{\vert i_t \vert \cdot\vert \hat{i}_{t, k}\vert} \right] ^ \eta,
L000875	    \label{eq:score-function}
L000876	\end{equation}
L000877	
L000878	\noindent where $\text{score}_k$ is the unnormalized score of the $k$th particle, and $\eta$ is a hyperparameter governing the relative strength of the motion prior and the data likelihood. Intuitively, a higher value of $\eta$ increases the separation between lower-valued and higher-valued particles, indicating that the algorithm should rely on the data likelihood more than the motion prior. When $\eta$ is set to be lower, the algorithm will not favor one particle significantly more than the other, reducing the impact of the data likelihood. This case is useful when individual measurements are less reliable or more noisy. While $\eta$ could be defined adaptively (e.g. based on per-frame SNR), we set $\eta$ to be fixed within an experiment. Once the score is computed for all particles, the score is normalized to produce a valid probability distribution. Finally, we use residual resampling \cite{hol2006resampling} to maintain a balance between low-variance sampling and particle diversity. The pseudocode algorithm for object tracking and camera localization are provided in \cref{alg:tracking} and \cref{alg:localization} respectively.
